<urn:sha256:3eda836d811b3d4f0671e71cbaff92c4e6e5db7a6bde078b8ae4133ef9c8f43b> <urn:ledger:ns#hash> "sha256:3eda836d811b3d4f0671e71cbaff92c4e6e5db7a6bde078b8ae4133ef9c8f43b" .
<urn:sha256:3eda836d811b3d4f0671e71cbaff92c4e6e5db7a6bde078b8ae4133ef9c8f43b> <urn:ledger:ns#ofDecision> <urn:dec:hafeok.ledger/01KZRBQ3YVCY8NJDR5RJ7QYFDK> .
<urn:sha256:3eda836d811b3d4f0671e71cbaff92c4e6e5db7a6bde078b8ae4133ef9c8f43b> <urn:ledger:ns#set> <urn:ledger-set:ledger-design> .
<urn:sha256:3eda836d811b3d4f0671e71cbaff92c4e6e5db7a6bde078b8ae4133ef9c8f43b> <urn:ledger:ns#statement> "Acceptance signing upgrades from claimed identity to certificate-based signatures at the next free spec revision — v1.4 / format 3, since L3 consumed v1.2 and v1.3 — additive, in the signature field L0 reserved. Five rulings: (1) the signature covers a canonical acceptance payload — decision id, version hash, actor identity, signing timestamp — detached, never a commit signature: an acceptance must attest that a named human bound themselves to a specific version hash, and a payload signature survives export, replay into another substrate, and entry movement, where a commit signature attests only that someone committed; L009 is unchanged and independent, blame attesting authorship while the signature attests acceptance. (2) Formats are git's own gpg.format trio — openpgp, ssh, x509 — with X.509 the bridge to the org product, where Entra-issued certificates make dev-side and org-side identity one mechanism. (3) Tier-gated, not universal: signature required above the tolerance floor (T2 signed; T1 claimed identity from git config as today), the dev-side analogue of the org product's step-up auth derived from the same OD-4 floor mechanism — and because the floor is up-only, the signature requirement cannot be shopped out of either. (4) The trust root — an allowed-signers set recording which keys are authorised for which identity — is a governed artifact: itself a set of decisions, living in the ledger. The bootstrap regress does not close: the first trust-root entry is an explicitly-named genesis entry, self-attested and conspicuous, never disguised as ordinary. (5) Validity is judged at the recorded signing timestamp, so routine key rotation never retroactively invalidates history; a since-revoked key raises a review finding — acceptances exist under a since-revoked key — never auto-invalidation. This is OD-3's scheduled upgrade path, not a reversal: claimed identity was always the L0 answer and the reserved field was the planned door. Basis, typed honestly: ruling 1 rests on a constraint — a commit signature structurally cannot bind a named human to a version hash across substrates — while the mechanism choices in rulings 2–4 rest on a stated preference for git-native signing and Entra-aligned org identity; no measured claim exists here and none is manufactured. Open sub-questions for the L6 session: whether the signature payload reuses the format's canonicalisation or takes a distinct domain-separation prefix; where allowed-signers entries live in the store layout; whether X.509 chain validation runs in-process or shells out to git." .
<urn:sha256:3eda836d811b3d4f0671e71cbaff92c4e6e5db7a6bde078b8ae4133ef9c8f43b> <urn:ledger:ns#toleranceOverride> "T2" .
