<urn:sha256:795c0fe177f378790b918b6e37abccee087f52b0523d1a626ea19318f623bc55> <urn:ledger:ns#hash> "sha256:795c0fe177f378790b918b6e37abccee087f52b0523d1a626ea19318f623bc55" .
<urn:sha256:795c0fe177f378790b918b6e37abccee087f52b0523d1a626ea19318f623bc55> <urn:ledger:ns#ofDecision> <urn:dec:hafeok.ledger/01KZXJX693301CZSY4XNP643XY> .
<urn:sha256:795c0fe177f378790b918b6e37abccee087f52b0523d1a626ea19318f623bc55> <urn:ledger:ns#set> <urn:ledger-set:ledger-design> .
<urn:sha256:795c0fe177f378790b918b6e37abccee087f52b0523d1a626ea19318f623bc55> <urn:ledger:ns#statement> "ledger accept --group|--set batches the act, never the signature. One invocation produces N acceptance records, one per decision, each signing its own version hash under scope: version — never one record covering N decisions, and never a hash ranging over members. What is batched is the invocation, which is an ergonomic affordance; the binding is untouched, and every acceptance the verb produces is exactly as bound as a single accept is. Six rulings make that safe rather than merely stated. (1) The selection is enumerated and pinned, never a predicate evaluated at write time: the selector is the same one ledger show resolves — the plan is built from show's screens rather than a second traversal, so show --group X and accept --group X cannot resolve to different sets — and it produces an explicit list of (decision, version hash, standing) triples. (2) Dry run is the default posture: the verb prints the enumeration and writes nothing; signing requires --confirm carrying the manifest hash that read produced, a value nobody types by accident because only a dry run can produce it, which is also why confirm-without-a-prior-read has no spelling. (3) A run refuses on drift: the manifest is a digest over exactly the selected triples under its own domain-separation prefix, standing included, so an acceptance filed between the read and the run moves it as surely as a revision does; a mismatching run refuses and names what moved, recovering the earlier selection by replaying the append-only log's own prefixes rather than caching anything between the two invocations. (4) A held member stops the whole batch by name — held is derived from the record, a basis pointer in the indeterminate: scheme saying the ground is unsettled, never a hand-listed exclusion that goes stale; a batch signing around it would make that judgment silently. (5) The per-member gate is unchanged: each acceptance runs the same delta-gate a single accept runs, once alone so a failure is attributed to its decision and once combined so the write is checked as the act it is, with no batch-only leniency of any kind. (6) Identity and committer semantics are unchanged: git config identity, no --as, L009 corroboration on every record produced. The verb deliberately does not touch the L1 precommitment tension: it signs present members, enumerated at run time, and no hash it produces ranges over a future member of a class — scope: class remains unsettled. Interim status, stated so it does not quietly become the answer: this is the CLI standing in for the acceptance workbench (L5), the same way the PR-review surface stands in for it elsewhere. Basis, typed honestly: the ruling is a mandate, and two structural constraints do the rest of the work — no hash ranges over more than one version, and a selection re-evaluated between the read and the run can sign an entry nobody read. No measured claim exists here and none is manufactured." .
